  "problem": "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA={{a}_{n}\\,:\\,{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-5\\,,\\,{a}_{1}=0,\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following sets is equivalent to $A?$",
  "worked_solution": "The elements of $A$ are all the terms of the *recursive* sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-5,\\,{a}_{1}=0,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nLet's write down the first few terms of our sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}-5 \\\\  & =0-5 \\\\  & =-5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}-5 \\\\  & =-5-5 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{3}-5 \\\\  & =-10-5 \\\\  & =-15 \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{4}-5 \\\\  & =-15-5 \\\\  & =-20 \\\\  & \u22ee\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA={0,-5,-10,-15,-20,\u2026}.\n$$",
